    <h1>About Freightgcc</h1>
    <p class="article-meta">By <a href="/author/wei-wong/">Wei Wong</a> · Independent editorial analysis · Last reviewed 16 November 2026</p>

    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>Freightgcc is an independent editorial project about moving goods from China to the six GCC markets: Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Qatar, Kuwait, Oman and Bahrain. It covers ports, routes, customs, certification, sectors and the decisions between them.</p>
    <p>It is not a forwarder, carrier, customs broker or marketplace. It does not sell freight, take bookings or earn commissions, so the analysis is not shaped by a carrier's margin or a forwarder's network.</p>

    <h2>Who publishes it, and who writes it</h2>
    <p>Freightgcc was founded in <strong>October 2025</strong> and is published by <strong>YIWU DINGWEI TRADING COMPANY</strong>, whose business address is 3# Building, North Silicon Valley, Qiaodong District, Zhangjiakou City, Hebei, China. The company's D-U-N-S Number is <strong>585974555</strong>. <strong>Wei Wong</strong> is the author and editorial lead; the Freightgcc editorial desk is the publishing identity behind the site.</p>
    <p>Commercial enquiries are handled separately by Sales; editorial coverage is not for sale and a commercial team does not decide what is published. The standards, contact route and corrections record are the accountability surface for the editorial work.</p>
    <p>Wei Wong leads editorial review and checks every page against the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> before publication. Research and drafting are supported by AI tools under editorial direction; the byline identifies a responsible editor rather than an invented biography. Read the <a href="/author/wei-wong/">author page</a> for the role and disclosure.</p>

    <h2>What we cover</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Countries</strong> — the six GCC markets, their import rules and commercial logic.</li>
      <li><strong>Ports</strong> — the gateways that matter for China-origin cargo, from Jebel Ali to Salalah.</li>
      <li><strong>Guides</strong> — FCL, LCL, air, DDP, customs clearance, certification, cost and transit.</li>
      <li><strong>Sectors</strong> — electronics, batteries, chemicals, food, furniture, machinery, ecommerce.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare</strong> — which port, which mode, which market, who clears.</li>
      <li><strong>Updates and notes</strong> — dated briefings, and arguments about how the market works.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How topics are chosen, and what is left out</h2>
    <p>A subject earns a page when four things are true: someone has to decide something; it can be answered from documents, not opinion; the answer changes a cost, a date or a compliance obligation; and there is enough checkable material to survive verification.</p>
    <p>What that excludes is deliberate. Freightgcc does not cover breaking news, forwarder rankings, named-company investigations, individual tariff classifications, sanctions screening, insurance claims, immigration or labour law, or any market outside China-origin cargo into the GCC.</p>

    <h2>How the analysis is built</h2>
    <p>Each page starts with the operational question a shipper would ask: how long, how much, what can go wrong, who is responsible. Research then separates <strong>reported</strong> facts (cited official or trade sources), <strong>industry claims</strong> (ranges or practices attributed to forwarders, carriers or importers) and <strong>editorial judgement</strong> (our assessment, labelled and arguable). Mixing all three into one confident voice is easier to write and harder to trust.</p>

    <h2>Independence, funding and conflicts</h2>
    <p>Editorial coverage is not for sale: there are no sponsored posts, paid placements, affiliate links or commissions for a mention, ranking or recommendation. Commercial enquiries are handled by Sales under separate terms and do not buy editorial coverage. The site does not publish revenue or traffic figures; independence requires disclosing who pays, not what they pay.</p>
    <p>Independence is not neutrality — the desk takes positions and labels them as judgements. Conflicts are handled in the open: if the desk has a commercial or personal relationship with an organisation discussed on a page, either the page says so where it matters or the page is not published. A future funding source would be named here and on any page it touches before the content it funds appeared.</p>

    <h2>What we will not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>We will not quote a precise freight rate.</strong> Rates move weekly and depend on the shipment; we give planning ranges and the drivers.</li>
      <li><strong>We will not guarantee a transit time.</strong> We give ranges and explain what moves them.</li>
      <li><strong>We will not name clients or claim credentials we do not hold.</strong> Case material is anonymised or omitted.</li>
      <li><strong>We will not pass a scaffold off as a finished page.</strong> Pages without substantive content are noindex and kept out of the sitemap.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Freightgcc does not do</h2>
    <p>We do not forward, book, clear, insure, inspect, warehouse or transport goods. We do not give legal, tax or customs-brokerage advice on a specific shipment, and nothing here substitutes for a licensed broker or an authority ruling. We do not act as an agent in a dispute, rank or recommend named forwarders, sell data or leads, verify a company's creditworthiness, or arbitrate between parties.</p>
    <p>If you need those services you need a licensed provider locally; this site's job is to help you ask better questions. The <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> sets out the limits of what can be relied on here, and the <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a> covers what the site collects.</p>

    <h2>How the site is corrected</h2>
    <p>We make mistakes. We fix them on the page, note the fix in its update log, and record anything material in the <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>, which also sets out our response times and how to appeal a decision. Report an error through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. No error gets fixed quietly here.</p>

    <h2>How to cite Freightgcc</h2>
    <p>Cite the page, not the site:</p>
    <p><code>Freightgcc, "Title of the page", Freightgcc, last reviewed 15 November 2026, https://freightgcc.com/path-to-page/</code></p>
    <p>In text, <code>(Freightgcc, 2026)</code> is enough. Use the last-reviewed date printed on the page, not the date you read it. Quote a range with its as-of date, as a planning range rather than a price. If the page may change before your work is read, archive the version you used. Short quotation with attribution needs no permission; republishing a page in full is not allowed under the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.</p>

    <h2>How to use the site</h2>
    <p>Start with the market you care about: <a href="/guides/china-to-saudi-arabia/">Saudi Arabia</a>, the <a href="/guides/china-to-uae/">UAE</a>, <a href="/guides/china-to-qatar/">Qatar</a>, <a href="/guides/china-to-kuwait/">Kuwait</a>, <a href="/guides/china-to-oman/">Oman</a> or <a href="/guides/china-to-bahrain/">Bahrain</a>. Then the method: <a href="/guides/customs-clearance/">customs clearance</a>, <a href="/guides/certification/">certification</a>, <a href="/guides/ddp/">DDP</a> and <a href="/guides/cost-and-transit/">cost and transit</a>.</p>

    <h2>A note on scope</h2>
    <p>The site covers China-origin cargo into the GCC, not Europe, the Americas, Africa or the broader Middle East. That is deliberate: a smaller scope with deeper content beats a broad directory with shallow pages.</p>
    <p>It does not cover every port or product either: the ports are those that matter for China–GCC container, project and bulk cargo; the sectors are those with distinct compliance requirements. If a page is missing, the information is not yet good enough to publish, not because the topic is unimportant.</p>

